            0.152,
            0.262,
            0.447,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": "**Ans**. (i) In trapezium, PQ||RS"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.228,
            0.289,
            0.321,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{R} = 75^{\\circ}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_34_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.227,
            0.308,
            0.348,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle \\mathrm{S}=45^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_34_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.334,
            0.558,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": "(ii) In trapezium ABCD given AD = BC Then $\\angle \\mathrm{A} = 80^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.226,
            0.375,
            0.558,
            0.394
          ],
          "content": "Since $\\mathrm{AD} = \\mathrm{BC}.$ So trapezium"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.565,
            0.258,
            0.873,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_34_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.227,
            0.395,
            0.735,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "ABCD is an isosceles trapezium. Thus $\\angle \\mathrm{B} = \\angle \\mathrm{A} = 80^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.415,
            0.623,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_34_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.587,
            0.848,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": "4. Draw a Venn diagram showing the set of parallelograms, kites, rhombuses, rectangles, and squares. Then, answer the following questions—"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.647,
            0.847,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": "(i) What is the quadrilateral that is both a kite and a parallelogram?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.689,
            0.847,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Can there be a quadrilateral that is both a kite and a rectangle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.714,
            0.846,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Is every kite a rhombus? If not, what is the correct relationship between these two types of quadrilaterals?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.761,
            0.204,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": "**Ans**."
